<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- NuGet packaging metadata -->
    <Description>The cross-cutting half of a request: tracing, logging, metrics, authorization, validation, idempotency, transactions and auditing, composed in one fixed order and each opted into explicitly.

A handler decides business outcomes; everything around that decision lives here, so adding an audit trail or an idempotency guard later changes a marker interface on a command, never a handler body. The order is fixed by the package rather than by your registration calls, because it is a correctness property: authorization runs before validation so a caller who may not act never learns the validation rules, and a commit happens before a cache eviction so nothing evicts on the strength of a write that has not landed.

Expected failures are Result values, never exceptions. Authorization fails closed — a request declaring no permissions is denied. Only a successful outcome persists anything: a failed Result commits nothing, caches nothing, and releases its idempotency key. Only the outermost command commits, so a nested command joins the outer transaction, and ICommandScope.OnCompleted runs work strictly after that commit. Idempotency reserves a key with a request fingerprint, replays the original response for a completed duplicate, and rejects the same key sent with a different body.

Depends on SharedKernel.Application, SharedKernel.Primitives, MediatR 12.4.1, FluentValidation and first-party Microsoft.Extensions packages — no cache, no Polly, no hosting, enforced by an architecture test. Caching behaviors ship separately as SharedKernel.Application.Behaviors.Caching.</Description>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>First release.

WHAT IS IN IT
- Eight behaviors across five stages: TracingBehavior, LoggingBehavior, MetricsBehavior (Observability), AuthorizationBehavior (Authorization), ValidationBehavior (Validation), then a command scope, IdempotencyBehavior, TransactionBehavior and AuditingBehavior (Command). The Query and Command stages also accept your own behaviors.
- ApplicationBehaviorsBuilder: AddDefaultBehaviors() turns on the four that need no infrastructure; every other behavior is an individual opt-in whose seam Build() verifies at startup. AddBehavior(type, PipelineStage, requiredServices) places a service-specific behavior in the canonical order.
- Four local seams, bridged at your composition root: IRequestContext (in SharedKernel.Application), IUnitOfWork, IRequestIdempotencyStore and IAuditTrailWriter. That is what keeps this package free of persistence, security and cache dependencies.
- Request markers: IAuthorizeRequest, IIdempotentRequest, IAuditableRequest&lt;TResponse&gt;, ILoggableRequest&lt;TResponse&gt;.
- ICommandScope: nested-command detection plus OnCompleted, for work that must follow the commit.

THINGS WORTH KNOWING BEFORE YOU ADOPT IT
- Build() is not optional. The Add… calls only set flags, so a pipeline that was never built runs bare — the symptom is silence, not an error. Build() also refuses a second call on the same builder, and registers logging and metrics infrastructure itself.
- Validation returns a failure, it does not throw. Every failing field is collected into one Error.Validation(errors) carrying the set in Error.Details; 14.Presentation renders that as a 400 with a per-field errors map. Validators run sequentially, because two async validators sharing a scoped DbContext would throw.
- Authorization denies a request that declares no permissions (403), and returns 401 only when the caller is unauthenticated. Permission names never appear in the message a caller sees.
- The idempotency fingerprint defaults to a hash of the serialized request, which changes whenever the command type gains a property. Set IIdempotentRequest.Fingerprint on any command you expect to be retried across a deploy, or a legitimate retry comes back as idempotency.key_reused. A key is released on a business failure, so a corrected command can be resubmitted with the same key.
- Auditing records an attempt that throws as well as one that succeeds or is rejected; AuditEntry.ErrorCode carries the error code in the first case and the exception type name in the second. A failed audit write blocks the commit, except while handling a handler exception, where the original exception still wins.
- The request-duration histogram is in SECONDS (sharedkernel.application.request.duration, via IMeterFactory). A dashboard built on millisecond values needs retuning; 13.ServiceDefaults' WithApplicationTelemetry() registers matching buckets.
- EventIds 5100-5199. Spans are named after the request type and carry error.type/error.code on failure.

See the package README for the pipeline diagram, the failure matrix and the full reference.</PackageReleaseNotes>
